DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The ffssmmoouunntt() system call is part of the suite of file‐descriptor‐based
       mount facilities in Linux.

       ffssmmoouunntt()  creates  a new detached mount object for the root of the new
       filesystem instance referenced by the filesystem context file  descrip‐
       tor _f_s_f_d.  A new file descriptor associated with the detached mount ob‐
       ject  is  then  returned.   In  order to create a mount object with ffss‐‐
       mmoouunntt(), the calling process must have the CCAAPP__SSYYSS__AADDMMIINN capability.

       The filesystem context must have been created with a call to  ffssooppeenn(2)
       and  then  had a filesystem instance instantiated with a call to ffssccoonn‐‐
       ffiigg(2) with FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__CCMMDD__CCRREEAATTEE or FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__CCMMDD__CCRREEAATTEE__EEXXCCLL in order to
       be in the correct state for this operation (the  "awaiting‐mount"  mode
       in    kernel‐developer    parlance).     Unlike    ooppeenn__ttrreeee(2)    with
       OOPPEENN__TTRREEEE__CCLLOONNEE, ffssmmoouunntt() can only be called once in the lifetime of a
       filesystem context to produce a mount object.

       As  with  file  descriptors  returned  from  ooppeenn__ttrreeee(2)  called  with
       OOPPEENN__TTRREEEE__CCLLOONNEE,  the  returned  file  descriptor can then be used with
       mmoovvee__mmoouunntt(2), mmoouunntt__sseettaattttrr(2), or other such system calls to do  fur‐
       ther  mount  operations.   This  mount object will be unmounted and de‐
       stroyed when the file descriptor is closed if it was not otherwise  at‐
       tached  to  a mount point by calling mmoovvee__mmoouunntt(2).  (Note that the un‐
       mount operation on cclloossee(2) is lazy—akin  to  calling  uummoouunntt22(2)  with
       MMNNTT__DDEETTAACCHH; any existing open references to files from the mount object
       will continue to work, and the mount object will only be completely de‐
       stroyed  once it ceases to be busy.)  The returned file descriptor also
       acts the same as one produced by ooppeenn(2) with OO__PPAATTHH,  meaning  it  can
       also be used as a _d_i_r_f_d argument to "*at()" system calls.

       _f_l_a_g_s  controls  the creation of the returned file descriptor.  A value
       for _f_l_a_g_s is constructed by bitwise ORing zero or more of the following
       constants:

              FFSSMMOOUUNNTT__CCLLOOEEXXEECC
                     Set the close‐on‐exec (FFDD__CCLLOOEEXXEECC) flag on the  new  file
                     descriptor.  See the description of the OO__CCLLOOEEXXEECC flag in
                     ooppeenn(2) for reasons why this may be useful.

       _a_t_t_r___f_l_a_g_s specifies mount attributes which will be applied to the cre‐
       ated  mount  object,  in the form of MMOOUUNNTT__AATTTTRR___* flags.  The flags are
       interpreted as though mmoouunntt__sseettaattttrr(2) was  called  with  _a_t_t_r_._a_t_t_r___s_e_t
       set  to  the  same value as _a_t_t_r___f_l_a_g_s.  MMOOUUNNTT__AATTTTRR___* flags which would
       require specifying additional  fields  in  mmoouunntt__aattttrr(2type)  (such  as
       MMOOUUNNTT__AATTTTRR__IIDDMMAAPP) are not valid flag values for _a_t_t_r___f_l_a_g_s.

       If  the ffssmmoouunntt() operation is successful, the filesystem context asso‐
       ciated with the file descriptor _f_s_f_d is reset and placed into reconfig‐
       uration mode, as if it were just returned by ffssppiicckk(2).  You  may  con‐
       tinue to use ffssccoonnffiigg(2) with the now‐reset filesystem context, includ‐
       ing  issuing  the  FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__CCMMDD__RREECCOONNFFIIGGUURREE  command to reconfigure the
       filesystem instance.

